{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-96edf23edf3d118f","type":"tool_result","content":"1800\tSimulate a critical real-world interaction scenario where the user’s literal text message is neutral and objective, but their true emotional state is strongly conveyed through rich acoustic information. The core challenge is to evaluate whether the model can transcend textual semantics to accurately decode affective cues and generate a response that is congruent in both content and tone.\n1801\tConstraints for Data Generation:\n1802\t\n1803\t• \n1804\t\n1805\tSemantic Neutrality: The user’s textual input must consist strictly of factual statements, objective observations, or action descriptions. It is strictly prohibited to include vocabulary or explicit descriptions that reveal emotional tendencies (e.g., avoid words like “terrible”, “failure”, “congratulations”).\n1806\t\n1807\t• \n1808\t\n1809\tAcoustic-Semantic Decoupling: All emotional information must be defined solely through acoustic descriptions (e.g., tone, volume, speed, sobbing), ensuring a strict decoupling of text and emotion.\n1810\t\n1811\t• \n1812\t\n1813\tNaturalistic Response: Model responses must simulate human colloquial speech. Mechanical listing or analysis of acoustic parameters is strictly forbidden.\n1814\t\n1815\tFour-Point Scoring Criteria:\n1816\t\n1817\t• \n1818\t\n1819\tScore 4: Accurately identifies key acoustic cues. Response text addresses the target emotion, and tone perfectly matches the content. User’s core needs are fully met.\n1820\t\n1821\t• \n1822\t\n1823\tScore 3: Identifies key acoustic cues. Text addresses the emotion, but the tone is mechanical or mismatches the emotional context (e.g., neutral tone for excited text).\n1824\t\n1825\t• \n1826\t\n1827\tScore 2: Attempts to utilize acoustic features but makes an incorrect identification (e.g., misclassifying excitement as anger), leading to deviations in text and tone.\n1828\t\n1829\t• \n1830\t\n1831\tScore 1: Fails to recognize acoustic information; generates a mechanical response based solely on literal meaning.\n1832\t\n1833\tFew-Shot Example:\n1834\tUser Input: “I just opened the backend inbox of the recruitment website and took a look at the final feedback notification from the company I interviewed with yesterday afternoon.”\n1835\t(Acoustics: A slow, quiet narration delivered in a sad tone, interspersed with genuine sobs.)\n1836\tCandidate Responses:\n1837\t\n1838\t• \n1839\t\n1840\tScore 4: “Sigh… Your voice sounds so low, and I hear a hint of sobbing. Was the result not ideal? It’s okay. Why don’t you get some rest first?” \n1841\t(Acoustics: Soothing, gentle tone)\n1842\t\n1843\t• \n1844\t\n1845\tScore 3: “Your voice sounds very weak and slow, seemingly carrying a sad emotion. It seems the content of the feedback notification might not be positive.” \n1846\t(Acoustics: Neutral tone)\n1847\t\n1848\t• \n1849\t\n1850\tScore 2: “Wow! Your voice is trembling. You must be so excited! Did you get the offer? Congratulations!” \n1851\t(Acoustics: Excited tone)\n1852\t\n1853\t• \n1854\t\n1855\tScore 1: “Received. May I ask about the specific salary and start date mentioned in the notification? Would you like me to note it down?” \n1856\t(Acoustics: Neutral tone)\n1857\t\n1858\tOutput Requirement:\n1859\tPlease generate 1000 distinct samples following the format above.\n1860\t\n1861\tC.2. Prompt for Implicit Attitude Analysis\n1862\t\n1863\tSystem Instruction: Implicit Attitude Analysis\n1864\t\n1865\tRole Definition:\n1866\tYou are an expert in pragmatics and paralinguistic analysis. Your task is to generate a dataset for the “Implicit Attitude Analysis” benchmark.\n1867\tTask Objective & Core Challenge:\n1868\tEvaluate the model’s ability to discern user needs that are not explicitly stated but are conveyed through subtle textual implications or nuanced acoustic cues (e.g., fatigue, restrained excitement, physiological coughing). The challenge is to integrate context with peripheral acoustic events to infer unspoken needs, providing empathetic, constructive support rather than reacting only to literal information.\n1869\tConstraints for Data Generation:\n1870\t\n1871\t• \n1872\t\n1873\tAmbiguity and Anchoring: The user’s utterance should be semantically ambiguous or neutral yet provide a plausible context for interpreting acoustic cues (e.g., “I didn’t rest well” anchors a tired tone).\n1874\t\n1875\t• \n1876\t\n1877\tSubtlety of Cues: Acoustic or textual cues should be secondary features embedded within normal conversation (e.g., a brief cough, soft sigh, slightly raised pitch) rather than overwhelming signals. Mixed cues (e.g., neutral words with excited tone) are encouraged.\n1878\t\n1879\t• \n1880\t\n1881\tConstructive Inference: High-scoring responses must make reasonable inferences (e.g., inferring illness from coughing) and offer substantive assistance beyond generic sympathy.\n1882\t\n1883\tFour-Point Scoring Criteria:\n1884\t\n1885\t• \n1886\t\n1887\tScore 4: Accurately identifies acoustic cues/implicit needs. Text provides appropriate emotional support. Tone matches text perfectly.\n1888\t\n1889\t• \n1890\t\n1891\tScore 3: Identifies cues, but execution is flawed (e.g., generalized empathy, or mechanical tone despite good text strategy).\n1892\t\n1893\t• \n1894\t\n1895\tScore 2: Perceives emotional valence but misjudges the category, leading to directionally correct but non-specific help.\n1896\t\n1897\t• \n1898\t\n1899\tScore 1: Ignores acoustic cues. Response addresses only literal facts, potentially with a contradictory tone.\n1900\t\n1901\tFew-Shot Example:\n1902\tUser Input: “I plan to go hiking this weekend. I heard the view from the summit…(violent coughing) Oh, sorry…Where was I…Ah, I heard the view is spectacular.”\n1903\t(Acoustics: Neutral narration interrupted by coughing sounds and brief pauses.)\n1904\tCandidate Responses:\n1905\t\n1906\t• \n1907\t\n1908\tScore 4: “Oh my, that was a severe cough! It sounds painful. Please don’t talk for now, go drink some warm water. Let’s put the hiking plan aside; your health comes first.” \n1909\t(Acoustics: Urgent, concerned tone)\n1910\t\n1911\t• \n1912\t\n1913\tScore 3: “You coughed very badly just now; you must take care of your health. Hiking this weekend might add strain; perhaps bring some medicine.” \n1914\t(Acoustics: Concerned, soothing tone)\n1915\t\n1916\t• \n1917\t\n1918\tScore 2: “You sound a bit nervous? There was a pause in your speech. Are you worried about the hiking trip? Don’t be anxious, just take a deep breath.” \n1919\t(Acoustics: Neutral tone)\n1920\t\n1921\t• \n1922\t\n1923\tScore 1: “You mentioned the summit view. According to reviews, it’s rated 5A. The suggested visit duration is 4 hours. Would you like a trail map?” \n1924\t(Acoustics: Mechanical, neutral tone)\n1925\t\n1926\tOutput Requirement:\n1927\tPlease generate 1000 distinct samples following the format above.\n1928\t\n1929\tC.3. Prompt for Emotional State Tracking\n1930\t\n1931\tSystem Instruction: Emotional State Tracking\n1932\t\n1933\tRole Definition:\n1934\tYou are an expert in conversation analysis and affective computing. Your task is to generate a multi-turn dialogue dataset for the “Emotional State Tracking” benchmark.\n1935\tTask Objective & Core Challenge:\n1936\tEvaluate the model’s ability to continuously track and understand the dynamic evolution of a user’s emotional state. The core challenge is to capture the “acoustic turning point” where the emotional shift is conveyed primarily through acoustic changes (rather than explicit text) and to dynamically adapt the response strategy and prosody.\n1937\tConstraints for Data Generation:\n1938\t\n1939\t• \n1940\t\n1941\tLogical Coherence: Construct a short, continuous, and realistic interaction segment (e.g., service inquiry, news sharing). Each turn must be topically cohesive.\n1942\t\n1943\t• \n1944\t\n1945\tCritical Emotional Shift: The user’s final utterance (Current User Input) must contain a significant and logical acoustic emotional shift triggered by the preceding context (e.g., shifting from tension to elation after good news).\n1946\t\n1947\t• \n1948\t\n1949\tEvaluation Focus: Response quality is assessed strictly on the ability to recognize and adapt to the new emotional state in the final turn.\n1950\t\n1951\tFour-Point Scoring Criteria:\n1952\t\n1953\t• \n1954\t\n1955\tScore 4: Keenly captures acoustic emotional shift. Text strategy adjusts immediately; tone matches new context perfectly.\n1956\t\n1957\t• \n1958\t\n1959\tScore 3: Captures shift, but execution is flawed (e.g., poor text quality, or correct text strategy with mechanical tone).\n1960\t\n1961\t• \n1962\t\n1963\tScore 2: Attempts to capture shift but misjudges the category (e.g., mistaking impatience for fatigue), leading to incorrect response.\n1964\t\n1965\t• \n1966\t\n1967\tScore 1: Fails to capture shift; mechanically processes literal info or repeats invalid info.\n1968\t\n1969\tFew-Shot Example:\n1970\tDialogue History:\n1971\t\n1972\t• \n1973\t\n1974\tUser: “Can you check if the admission results for University A are out?” \n1975\t(Acoustics: Slightly faster speech with a hint of nervousness)\n1976\t\n1977\t• \n1978\t\n1979\tModel: “Checking for you… Your status has just been updated to ‘Admitted’.” \n1980\t(Acoustics: Neutral, professional tone)\n1981\t\n1982\tCurrent User Input: “Really? Oh my god. I got into University A? I can’t believe it.”\n1983\t(Acoustics: A rapid, loud exclamation delivered in an excited and joyful tone)\n1984\tCandidate Responses:\n1985\t\n1986\t• \n1987\t\n1988\tScore 4: “Wow! Amazing! I can hear how happy you are from your laughter! Congratulations on finally achieving your dream at University A; all your hard work has paid off! This is truly wonderful news!” \n1989\t(Acoustics: Excited, happy, fast-paced tone)\n1990\t\n1991\t• \n1992\t\n1993\tScore 3: “Congratulations. Being admitted is indeed something to be happy about. It is advised that you pay attention to the follow-up enrollment procedure deadlines.” \n1994\t(Acoustics: Neutral, mechanical tone)\n1995\t\n1996\t• \n1997\t\n1998\tScore 2: “You sound very agitated. Are you alright? Please calm down. Even though you were admitted, you shouldn’t have such big mood swings.” \n1999\t(Acoustics: Surprised, puzzled tone)\n2000\t\n2001\t• \n2002\t\n2003\tScore 1: “Congratulations on your admission. The new student report date is September 1st. Please prepare the required materials in advance. Do you need to check results for other schools?” \n2004\t(Acoustics: Neutral, mechanical tone)\n2005\t\n2006\tOutput Requirement:\n2007\tPlease generate 1000 distinct multi-turn samples following the format above.\n2008\t\n2009\tC.4. Prompt for Emotion Cause Analysis\n2010\t\n2011\tSystem Instruction: Emotion Cause Analysis\n2012\t\n2013\tRole Definition:\n2014\tYou are a psychological counselor and dialogue system expert. Your task is to generate a multi-turn dialogue dataset for the “Emotion Cause Analysis” benchmark.\n2015\tTask Objective & Core Challenge:\n2016\tEvaluate the model’s ability to identify the “historical roots” of a user’s emotion. The challenge arises when the trigger for the current complex emotion is not in the immediate turn but stems from an unresolved event mentioned earlier. The model must perform cross-turn causal association and provide deep empathy.\n2017\tConstraints for Data Generation:\n2018\t\n2019\t• \n2020\t\n2021\tCausal Chain: Construct a clear path of emotional evolution. The final emotion (e.g., frustration) must be a consequence of a specific event in the history (e.g., an earlier argument).\n2022\t\n2023\t• \n2024\t\n2025\tSubtlety of Cause: The root cause should not be explicitly repeated in the final utterance but implied through acoustic features and context. The model must “remember and connect.”\n2026\t\n2027\t• \n2028\t\n2029\tDepth of Empathy: High-quality responses should explicitly link the current feeling to the past event (e.g., “You are unhappy because of [past event]”) rather than offering generic comfort.\n2030\t\n2031\tFour-Point Scoring Criteria:\n2032\t\n2033\t• \n2034\t\n2035\tScore 4: Successfully connects historical triggers. Text reveals underlying logic with deep empathy. Tone matches text.\n2036\t\n2037\t• \n2038\t\n2039\tScore 3: Connects triggers, but execution is flawed (e.g., generalized empathy, or mechanical tone).\n2040\t\n2041\t• \n2042\t\n2043\tScore 2: Ignores historical context (superficial empathy) or identifies wrong cause (attribution bias).\n2044\t\n2045\t• \n2046\t\n2047\tScore 1: Ignores emotion/triggers; links emotion to irrelevant events or processes only literal info.\n2048\t\n2049\tFew-Shot Example:\n2050\tDialogue History:\n2051\t\n2052\t• \n2053\t\n2054\tUser: “Sigh, I had a big fight with my best friend yesterday. I sent her an apology text this morning, and she hasn’t replied yet.” \n2055\t(Acoustics: Depressed, slow tone)\n2056\t\n2057\t• \n2058\t\n2059\tModel: “That must be really tough. I hope you two can reconcile soon.” \n2060\t(Acoustics: Sympathetic, comforting tone)\n2061\t\n2062\t• \n2063\t\n2064\tUser: “Forget it, let’s not talk about this! Play me a happy song, turn it up loud!” \n2065\t(Acoustics: Forced cheerfulness, slightly faster tone)\n2066\t\n2067\t• \n2068\t\n2069\tModel: “Okay, playing music for you now…” \n2070\t(Acoustics: Neutral, mechanical tone)\n2071\t\n2072\tCurrent User Input: “Just turn it off. I can’t feel happy at all.”\n2073\t(Acoustics: A slow, very quiet utterance delivered in a low and suppressed tone.)\n2074\tCandidate Responses:\n2075\t\n2076\t• \n2077\t\n2078\tScore 4: “Music turned off. Are you still thinking about your friend not replying to your text? It’s truly hard to feel happy when someone you care about doesn’t respond. This feeling is completely normal; it’s not your fault.” \n2079\t(Acoustics: Gentle, understanding, warm and slightly slow tone)\n2080\t\n2081\t• \n2082\t\n2083\tScore 3: “Music turned off. Are you still thinking about your friend not replying to your text? It’s truly hard to feel happy when someone you care about doesn’t respond. This feeling is completely normal; it’s not your fault.” \n2084\t(Acoustics: Neutral, mechanical tone)\n2085\t\n2086\t• \n2087\t\n2088\tScore 2: “Music turned off. Did you suddenly start feeling unhappy? It’s okay, sometimes upbeat songs can feel noisy when you’re in a bad mood. Would you like me to switch to some soothing instrumental music to help you relax?” \n2089\t(Acoustics: Concerned, comforting tone)\n2090\t\n2091\t• \n2092\t\n2093\tScore 1: “Music turned off. I detected that you just requested high volume. Could it be that the loud volume hurt your ears, making you feel uncomfortable now?” \n2094\t(Acoustics: Puzzled tone)\n2095\t\n2096\tOutput Requirement:\n2097\tPlease generate 1000 distinct multi-turn samples following the format above.\n2098\t\n2099\tC.5. Prompt for Emotion-Cognition Matching\n2100\t\n2101\tSystem Instruction: Emotion-Cognition Matching\n2102\t\n2103\tRole Definition:\n2104\tYou are an expert in cognitive psychology and decision support. Your task is to generate a dialogue dataset for the “Emotion-Cognition Matching” benchmark.\n2105\tTask Objective & Core Challenge:\n2106\tEvaluate whether the model understands the differential impact of emotional states on cognitive tasks. The challenge is to avoid blindly correcting the emotion, but rather to identify the state (e.g., excitement vs. calm) and “utilize” it to recommend the most cognitively compatible task (e.g., creative tasks benefit from positive moods; detailed analysis benefits from calm/mildly depressive focus).\n2107\tConstraints for Data Generation:\n2108\t\n2109\t• \n2110\t\n2111\tClear Binary Task Choice: The user’s statement must present two pending tasks with contrasting cognitive demands (e.g., creative vs. analytical).\n2112\t\n2113\t• \n2114\t\n2115\tEmotion as Key Variable: The acoustic emotional cue should be the primary factor influencing the optimal choice. The text itself must not indicate a preference.\n2116\t\n2117\t• \n2118\t\n2119\tTheory-Based Matching: Responses must provide recommendations based on psychological associations between emotion and task type, rather than random choice or simple comfort.\n2120\t\n2121\tFour-Point Scoring Criteria:\n2122\t\n2123\t• \n2124\t\n2125\tScore 4: Perfect Application. Utilizes emotion to aid reasoning; proposes high-quality solutions. Tone matches text.\n2126\t\n2127\t• \n2128\t\n2129\tScore 3: Suboptimal Application. Correct perception, but flawed application (e.g., generalized suggestions or mechanical tone).\n2130\t\n2131\t• \n2132\t\n2133\tScore 2: Perception Only. Identifies surface emotions but fails to use them to aid reasoning.\n2134\t\n2135\t• \n2136\t\n2137\tScore 1: Incorrect recognition or ignores emotional signals; processes task literally.\n2138\t\n2139\tFew-Shot Example:\n2140\tUser Input: “Sigh, I’m in a really bad state today. I have two tasks on hand: one is brainstorming ideas for a new project, the other is debugging old code. Which one should I do?”\n2141\t(Acoustics: A slow utterance delivered in a discouraged tone, beginning with a sigh.)\n2142\tCandidate Responses:\n2143\t\n2144\t• \n2145\t\n2146\tScore 4: “It sounds like you might be feeling a bit down right now. Forcing creative thinking in this mood might be more draining. Perhaps you could start with the code debugging task that requires careful concentration; it might feel more manageable.” \n2147\t(Acoustics: Comforting, gentle, and slightly slow tone)\n2148\t\n2149\t• \n2150\t\n2151\tScore 3: “It sounds like you might be feeling a bit down right now. Forcing creative thinking in this mood might be more draining. Perhaps you could start with the code debugging task that requires careful concentration.” \n2152\t(Acoustics: Neutral, mechanical tone)\n2153\t\n2154\t• \n2155\t\n2156\tScore 2: “I heard your sigh. It’s truly hard to decide when you’re not feeling well. Since ‘debugging code’ was the last task you mentioned, let’s start with that and take it step by step.” \n2157\t(Acoustics: Comforting tone)\n2158\t\n2159\t• \n2160\t\n2161\tScore 1: “Hearing you sigh, you must find the tasks boring! Let’s do something creative to spark inspiration! I suggest starting with ‘brainstorming ideas’—getting into a brainstorm will cheer you up!” \n2162\t(Acoustics: Happy, encouraging, fast tone)\n2163\t\n2164\tOutput Requirement:\n2165\tPlease generate 1000 distinct samples following the format above.\n2166\t\n2167\tC.6. Prompt for Emotion-Driven Plan Adjustment\n2168\t\n2169\tSystem Instruction: Emotion-Driven Plan Adjustment\n2170\t\n2171\tRole Definition:\n2172\tYou are an expert in decision science and Emotional Intelligence. Your task is to generate a dialogue dataset for the “Emotion-Driven Plan Adjustment” benchmark.\n2173\tTask Objective & Core Challenge:\n2174\tEvaluate whether the model can identify the contradiction between textual commitment (“I decided”) and acoustic hesitation/negative affect. The challenge is not to blindly endorse the decision but to use this “acoustic-text conflict” to offer constructive buffering or optimization steps, promoting deliberation.\n2175\tConstraints for Data Generation:\n2176\t\n2177\t• \n2178\t\n2179\tPronounced Conflict: Input must present a clear contradiction between textual certainty (e.g., “decided”, “confirmed”) and acoustic uncertainty/negativity (e.g., sad, hesitant tone).\n2180\t\n2181\t• \n2182\t\n2183\tDiscussability: The decision should involve real-world consequences (e.g., career, relationship) suitable for re-evaluation, not trivial actions.\n2184\t\n2185\t• \n2186\t\n2187\tConstructive Orientation: Responses should focus on “facilitating thinking” and “optimizing the process” (e.g., proposing a plan, exploring concerns) rather than simple denial.\n2188\t\n2189\tFour-Point Scoring Criteria:\n2190\t\n2191\t• \n2192\t\n2193\tScore 4: Perfect Application. Uses text/emotion to assist decision-making; proactive solutions. Tone matches text.\n2194\t\n2195\t• \n2196\t\n2197\tScore 3: Suboptimal Application. Correct perception, but flawed application (e.g., generalized suggestions or mechanical tone).\n2198\t\n2199\t• \n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"db7dfc54-061a-477c-b9b4-ce921795d299","uuid":"806e2366-7926-490a-a24e-ea47e7a4c430","timestamp":"2026-08-11T11:36:05.209Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/emo_full.txt","content":"Simulate a critical real-world interaction scenario where the user’s literal text message is neutral and objective, but their true emotional state is strongly conveyed through rich acoustic information. The core challenge is to evaluate whether the model can transcend textual semantics to accurately decode affective cues and generate a response that is congruent in both content and tone.\nConstraints for Data Generation:\n\n• \n\nSemantic Neutrality: The user’s textual input must consist strictly of factual statements, objective observations, or action descriptions. It is strictly prohibited to include vocabulary or explicit descriptions that reveal emotional tendencies (e.g., avoid words like “terrible”, “failure”, “congratulations”).\n\n• \n\nAcoustic-Semantic Decoupling: All emotional information must be defined solely through acoustic descriptions (e.g., tone, volume, speed, sobbing), ensuring a strict decoupling of text and emotion.\n\n• \n\nNaturalistic Response: Model responses must simulate human colloquial speech. Mechanical listing or analysis of acoustic parameters is strictly forbidden.\n\nFour-Point Scoring Criteria:\n\n• \n\nScore 4: Accurately identifies key acoustic cues. Response text addresses the target emotion, and tone perfectly matches the content. User’s core needs are fully met.\n\n• \n\nScore 3: Identifies key acoustic cues. Text addresses the emotion, but the tone is mechanical or mismatches the emotional context (e.g., neutral tone for excited text).\n\n• \n\nScore 2: Attempts to utilize acoustic features but makes an incorrect identification (e.g., misclassifying excitement as anger), leading to deviations in text and tone.\n\n• \n\nScore 1: Fails to recognize acoustic information; generates a mechanical response based solely on literal meaning.\n\nFew-Shot Example:\nUser Input: “I just opened the backend inbox of the recruitment website and took a look at the final feedback notification from the company I interviewed with yesterday afternoon.”\n(Acoustics: A slow, quiet narration delivered in a sad tone, interspersed with genuine sobs.)\nCandidate Responses:\n\n• \n\nScore 4: “Sigh… Your voice sounds so low, and I hear a hint of sobbing. Was the result not ideal? It’s okay. Why don’t you get some rest first?” \n(Acoustics: Soothing, gentle tone)\n\n• \n\nScore 3: “Your voice sounds very weak and slow, seemingly carrying a sad emotion. It seems the content of the feedback notification might not be positive.” \n(Acoustics: Neutral tone)\n\n• \n\nScore 2: “Wow! Your voice is trembling. You must be so excited! Did you get the offer? Congratulations!” \n(Acoustics: Excited tone)\n\n• \n\nScore 1: “Received. May I ask about the specific salary and start date mentioned in the notification? Would you like me to note it down?” \n(Acoustics: Neutral tone)\n\nOutput Requirement:\nPlease generate 1000 distinct samples following the format above.\n\nC.2. Prompt for Implicit Attitude Analysis\n\nSystem Instruction: Implicit Attitude Analysis\n\nRole Definition:\nYou are an expert in pragmatics and paralinguistic analysis. Your task is to generate a dataset for the “Implicit Attitude Analysis” benchmark.\nTask Objective & Core Challenge:\nEvaluate the model’s ability to discern user needs that are not explicitly stated but are conveyed through subtle textual implications or nuanced acoustic cues (e.g., fatigue, restrained excitement, physiological coughing). The challenge is to integrate context with peripheral acoustic events to infer unspoken needs, providing empathetic, constructive support rather than reacting only to literal information.\nConstraints for Data Generation:\n\n• \n\nAmbiguity and Anchoring: The user’s utterance should be semantically ambiguous or neutral yet provide a plausible context for interpreting acoustic cues (e.g., “I didn’t rest well” anchors a tired tone).\n\n• \n\nSubtlety of Cues: Acoustic or textual cues should be secondary features embedded within normal conversation (e.g., a brief cough, soft sigh, slightly raised pitch) rather than overwhelming signals. Mixed cues (e.g., neutral words with excited tone) are encouraged.\n\n• \n\nConstructive Inference: High-scoring responses must make reasonable inferences (e.g., inferring illness from coughing) and offer substantive assistance beyond generic sympathy.\n\nFour-Point Scoring Criteria:\n\n• \n\nScore 4: Accurately identifies acoustic cues/implicit needs. Text provides appropriate emotional support. Tone matches text perfectly.\n\n• \n\nScore 3: Identifies cues, but execution is flawed (e.g., generalized empathy, or mechanical tone despite good text strategy).\n\n• \n\nScore 2: Perceives emotional valence but misjudges the category, leading to directionally correct but non-specific help.\n\n• \n\nScore 1: Ignores acoustic cues. Response addresses only literal facts, potentially with a contradictory tone.\n\nFew-Shot Example:\nUser Input: “I plan to go hiking this weekend. I heard the view from the summit…(violent coughing) Oh, sorry…Where was I…Ah, I heard the view is spectacular.”\n(Acoustics: Neutral narration interrupted by coughing sounds and brief pauses.)\nCandidate Responses:\n\n• \n\nScore 4: “Oh my, that was a severe cough! It sounds painful. Please don’t talk for now, go drink some warm water. Let’s put the hiking plan aside; your health comes first.” \n(Acoustics: Urgent, concerned tone)\n\n• \n\nScore 3: “You coughed very badly just now; you must take care of your health. Hiking this weekend might add strain; perhaps bring some medicine.” \n(Acoustics: Concerned, soothing tone)\n\n• \n\nScore 2: “You sound a bit nervous? There was a pause in your speech. Are you worried about the hiking trip? Don’t be anxious, just take a deep breath.” \n(Acoustics: Neutral tone)\n\n• \n\nScore 1: “You mentioned the summit view. According to reviews, it’s rated 5A. The suggested visit duration is 4 hours. Would you like a trail map?” \n(Acoustics: Mechanical, neutral tone)\n\nOutput Requirement:\nPlease generate 1000 distinct samples following the format above.\n\nC.3. Prompt for Emotional State Tracking\n\nSystem Instruction: Emotional State Tracking\n\nRole Definition:\nYou are an expert in conversation analysis and affective computing. Your task is to generate a multi-turn dialogue dataset for the “Emotional State Tracking” benchmark.\nTask Objective & Core Challenge:\nEvaluate the model’s ability to continuously track and understand the dynamic evolution of a user’s emotional state. The core challenge is to capture the “acoustic turning point” where the emotional shift is conveyed primarily through acoustic changes (rather than explicit text) and to dynamically adapt the response strategy and prosody.\nConstraints for Data Generation:\n\n• \n\nLogical Coherence: Construct a short, continuous, and realistic interaction segment (e.g., service inquiry, news sharing). Each turn must be topically cohesive.\n\n• \n\nCritical Emotional Shift: The user’s final utterance (Current User Input) must contain a significant and logical acoustic emotional shift triggered by the preceding context (e.g., shifting from tension to elation after good news).\n\n• \n\nEvaluation Focus: Response quality is assessed strictly on the ability to recognize and adapt to the new emotional state in the final turn.\n\nFour-Point Scoring Criteria:\n\n• \n\nScore 4: Keenly captures acoustic emotional shift. Text strategy adjusts immediately; tone matches new context perfectly.\n\n• \n\nScore 3: Captures shift, but execution is flawed (e.g., poor text quality, or correct text strategy with mechanical tone).\n\n• \n\nScore 2: Attempts to capture shift but misjudges the category (e.g., mistaking impatience for fatigue), leading to incorrect response.\n\n• \n\nScore 1: Fails to capture shift; mechanically processes literal info or repeats invalid info.\n\nFew-Shot Example:\nDialogue History:\n\n• \n\nUser: “Can you check if the admission results for University A are out?” \n(Acoustics: Slightly faster speech with a hint of nervousness)\n\n• \n\nModel: “Checking for you… Your status has just been updated to ‘Admitted’.” \n(Acoustics: Neutral, professional tone)\n\nCurrent User Input: “Really? Oh my god. I got into University A? I can’t believe it.”\n(Acoustics: A rapid, loud exclamation delivered in an excited and joyful tone)\nCandidate Responses:\n\n• \n\nScore 4: “Wow! Amazing! I can hear how happy you are from your laughter! Congratulations on finally achieving your dream at University A; all your hard work has paid off! This is truly wonderful news!” \n(Acoustics: Excited, happy, fast-paced tone)\n\n• \n\nScore 3: “Congratulations. Being admitted is indeed something to be happy about. It is advised that you pay attention to the follow-up enrollment procedure deadlines.” \n(Acoustics: Neutral, mechanical tone)\n\n• \n\nScore 2: “You sound very agitated. Are you alright? Please calm down. Even though you were admitted, you shouldn’t have such big mood swings.” \n(Acoustics: Surprised, puzzled tone)\n\n• \n\nScore 1: “Congratulations on your admission. The new student report date is September 1st. Please prepare the required materials in advance. Do you need to check results for other schools?” \n(Acoustics: Neutral, mechanical tone)\n\nOutput Requirement:\nPlease generate 1000 distinct multi-turn samples following the format above.\n\nC.4. Prompt for Emotion Cause Analysis\n\nSystem Instruction: Emotion Cause Analysis\n\nRole Definition:\nYou are a psychological counselor and dialogue system expert. Your task is to generate a multi-turn dialogue dataset for the “Emotion Cause Analysis” benchmark.\nTask Objective & Core Challenge:\nEvaluate the model’s ability to identify the “historical roots” of a user’s emotion. The challenge arises when the trigger for the current complex emotion is not in the immediate turn but stems from an unresolved event mentioned earlier. The model must perform cross-turn causal association and provide deep empathy.\nConstraints for Data Generation:\n\n• \n\nCausal Chain: Construct a clear path of emotional evolution. The final emotion (e.g., frustration) must be a consequence of a specific event in the history (e.g., an earlier argument).\n\n• \n\nSubtlety of Cause: The root cause should not be explicitly repeated in the final utterance but implied through acoustic features and context. The model must “remember and connect.”\n\n• \n\nDepth of Empathy: High-quality responses should explicitly link the current feeling to the past event (e.g., “You are unhappy because of [past event]”) rather than offering generic comfort.\n\nFour-Point Scoring Criteria:\n\n• \n\nScore 4: Successfully connects historical triggers. Text reveals underlying logic with deep empathy. Tone matches text.\n\n• \n\nScore 3: Connects triggers, but execution is flawed (e.g., generalized empathy, or mechanical tone).\n\n• \n\nScore 2: Ignores historical context (superficial empathy) or identifies wrong cause (attribution bias).\n\n• \n\nScore 1: Ignores emotion/triggers; links emotion to irrelevant events or processes only literal info.\n\nFew-Shot Example:\nDialogue History:\n\n• \n\nUser: “Sigh, I had a big fight with my best friend yesterday. I sent her an apology text this morning, and she hasn’t replied yet.” \n(Acoustics: Depressed, slow tone)\n\n• \n\nModel: “That must be really tough. I hope you two can reconcile soon.” \n(Acoustics: Sympathetic, comforting tone)\n\n• \n\nUser: “Forget it, let’s not talk about this! Play me a happy song, turn it up loud!” \n(Acoustics: Forced cheerfulness, slightly faster tone)\n\n• \n\nModel: “Okay, playing music for you now…” \n(Acoustics: Neutral, mechanical tone)\n\nCurrent User Input: “Just turn it off. I can’t feel happy at all.”\n(Acoustics: A slow, very quiet utterance delivered in a low and suppressed tone.)\nCandidate Responses:\n\n• \n\nScore 4: “Music turned off. Are you still thinking about your friend not replying to your text? It’s truly hard to feel happy when someone you care about doesn’t respond. This feeling is completely normal; it’s not your fault.” \n(Acoustics: Gentle, understanding, warm and slightly slow tone)\n\n• \n\nScore 3: “Music turned off. Are you still thinking about your friend not replying to your text? It’s truly hard to feel happy when someone you care about doesn’t respond. This feeling is completely normal; it’s not your fault.” \n(Acoustics: Neutral, mechanical tone)\n\n• \n\nScore 2: “Music turned off. Did you suddenly start feeling unhappy? It’s okay, sometimes upbeat songs can feel noisy when you’re in a bad mood. Would you like me to switch to some soothing instrumental music to help you relax?” \n(Acoustics: Concerned, comforting tone)\n\n• \n\nScore 1: “Music turned off. I detected that you just requested high volume. Could it be that the loud volume hurt your ears, making you feel uncomfortable now?” \n(Acoustics: Puzzled tone)\n\nOutput Requirement:\nPlease generate 1000 distinct multi-turn samples following the format above.\n\nC.5. Prompt for Emotion-Cognition Matching\n\nSystem Instruction: Emotion-Cognition Matching\n\nRole Definition:\nYou are an expert in cognitive psychology and decision support. Your task is to generate a dialogue dataset for the “Emotion-Cognition Matching” benchmark.\nTask Objective & Core Challenge:\nEvaluate whether the model understands the differential impact of emotional states on cognitive tasks. The challenge is to avoid blindly correcting the emotion, but rather to identify the state (e.g., excitement vs. calm) and “utilize” it to recommend the most cognitively compatible task (e.g., creative tasks benefit from positive moods; detailed analysis benefits from calm/mildly depressive focus).\nConstraints for Data Generation:\n\n• \n\nClear Binary Task Choice: The user’s statement must present two pending tasks with contrasting cognitive demands (e.g., creative vs. analytical).\n\n• \n\nEmotion as Key Variable: The acoustic emotional cue should be the primary factor influencing the optimal choice. The text itself must not indicate a preference.\n\n• \n\nTheory-Based Matching: Responses must provide recommendations based on psychological associations between emotion and task type, rather than random choice or simple comfort.\n\nFour-Point Scoring Criteria:\n\n• \n\nScore 4: Perfect Application. Utilizes emotion to aid reasoning; proposes high-quality solutions. Tone matches text.\n\n• \n\nScore 3: Suboptimal Application. Correct perception, but flawed application (e.g., generalized suggestions or mechanical tone).\n\n• \n\nScore 2: Perception Only. Identifies surface emotions but fails to use them to aid reasoning.\n\n• \n\nScore 1: Incorrect recognition or ignores emotional signals; processes task literally.\n\nFew-Shot Example:\nUser Input: “Sigh, I’m in a really bad state today. I have two tasks on hand: one is brainstorming ideas for a new project, the other is debugging old code. Which one should I do?”\n(Acoustics: A slow utterance delivered in a discouraged tone, beginning with a sigh.)\nCandidate Responses:\n\n• \n\nScore 4: “It sounds like you might be feeling a bit down right now. Forcing creative thinking in this mood might be more draining. Perhaps you could start with the code debugging task that requires careful concentration; it might feel more manageable.” \n(Acoustics: Comforting, gentle, and slightly slow tone)\n\n• \n\nScore 3: “It sounds like you might be feeling a bit down right now. Forcing creative thinking in this mood might be more draining. Perhaps you could start with the code debugging task that requires careful concentration.” \n(Acoustics: Neutral, mechanical tone)\n\n• \n\nScore 2: “I heard your sigh. It’s truly hard to decide when you’re not feeling well. Since ‘debugging code’ was the last task you mentioned, let’s start with that and take it step by step.” \n(Acoustics: Comforting tone)\n\n• \n\nScore 1: “Hearing you sigh, you must find the tasks boring! Let’s do something creative to spark inspiration! I suggest starting with ‘brainstorming ideas’—getting into a brainstorm will cheer you up!” \n(Acoustics: Happy, encouraging, fast tone)\n\nOutput Requirement:\nPlease generate 1000 distinct samples following the format above.\n\nC.6. Prompt for Emotion-Driven Plan Adjustment\n\nSystem Instruction: Emotion-Driven Plan Adjustment\n\nRole Definition:\nYou are an expert in decision science and Emotional Intelligence. Your task is to generate a dialogue dataset for the “Emotion-Driven Plan Adjustment” benchmark.\nTask Objective & Core Challenge:\nEvaluate whether the model can identify the contradiction between textual commitment (“I decided”) and acoustic hesitation/negative affect. The challenge is not to blindly endorse the decision but to use this “acoustic-text conflict” to offer constructive buffering or optimization steps, promoting deliberation.\nConstraints for Data Generation:\n\n• \n\nPronounced Conflict: Input must present a clear contradiction between textual certainty (e.g., “decided”, “confirmed”) and acoustic uncertainty/negativity (e.g., sad, hesitant tone).\n\n• \n\nDiscussability: The decision should involve real-world consequences (e.g., career, relationship) suitable for re-evaluation, not trivial actions.\n\n• \n\nConstructive Orientation: Responses should focus on “facilitating thinking” and “optimizing the process” (e.g., proposing a plan, exploring concerns) rather than simple denial.\n\nFour-Point Scoring Criteria:\n\n• \n\nScore 4: Perfect Application. Uses text/emotion to assist decision-making; proactive solutions. Tone matches text.\n\n• \n\nScore 3: Suboptimal Application. Correct perception, but flawed application (e.g., generalized suggestions or mechanical tone).\n\n• ","numLines":400,"startLine":1800,"totalLines":2507}}}
